/* v4 learner-facing rewrites: Content (8/8 including n.what/n.author/n.audience in base file) */
Object.assign(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY||(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY={}),{
'n.nt':{title:'Know the shape of the New Testament before diving into details',dek:'The New Testament is a story of Jesus, a story of the early movement, letters into real communities, and one apocalyptic closing vision.',body:['The New Testament opens with four Gospels. They overlap in central events but present Jesus with different emphases. Acts then continues from resurrection/ascension into the early Church and the widening mission.','The letters make up most of the remaining shelf. They are not abstract theology textbooks; they address churches, leaders, conflicts, worship, ethics, teaching, suffering, money, status, inclusion, and hope. Paul’s letters form the largest collection, followed by Hebrews and the General Epistles.','Revelation closes the New Testament with apocalyptic prophecy addressed to churches. Its symbolic visions culminate in judgment on evil and renewed-creation imagery. Seeing these four broad kinds of material helps a new reader know what kind of book they have opened.'],plain:'Gospels tell Jesus’ story, Acts tells the movement’s spread, letters address communities, and Revelation closes with apocalyptic hope.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'The New Testament in four movements',nodes:[{title:'Gospels',text:'Four portraits of Jesus'},{title:'Acts',text:'The early movement spreads'},{title:'Letters',text:'Communities learn how to live the gospel'},{title:'Revelation',text:'Faithful endurance and renewed creation'}]},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Match the New Testament form to its job',prompt:'Connect each section with its broad function.',items:['Gospels','Acts','Letters','Revelation'],options:['Narrate Jesus’ ministry, death, and resurrection','Narrate the widening early Church','Address real community situations','Use apocalyptic visions to call for endurance and hope'],answer:[0,1,2,3],hints:['Acts is narrative; letters are correspondence.'],why:'The New Testament contains distinct literary blocks that do different work.'}},
'n.plot':{title:'A book summary should tell you the movement, not just list events',dek:'Good summaries reveal what changes from beginning to end and why those changes matter.',body:['A weak summary says “Genesis has creation, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and Joseph.” A stronger summary explains movement: Genesis begins with humanity as a whole, narrows to Abraham’s family, follows promise through four generations, and ends with that family in Egypt—setting up Exodus.','Plot does not only belong to narrative. A letter can have argumentative movement: problem, explanation, response, appeal. A prophetic book can move between accusation, judgment, lament, and hope. A wisdom book can stage a dispute. Summarizing means finding the logic that connects the parts.','A useful one-paragraph summary should help a learner predict where a passage belongs. If a summary is so vague that it could describe ten different books, it is not yet doing enough work.'],plain:'Do not summarize a book as a bag of topics. Explain how it moves and where it ends up.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Turn a list into a plot',nodes:[{title:'Starting situation',text:'Where does the book begin?'},{title:'Tension / question',text:'What problem drives it?'},{title:'Major movement',text:'What changes?'},{title:'Ending',text:'Where does it leave the reader?'},{title:'Bridge',text:'What does that set up next?'}]},challenge:{kind:'compare-board',title:'Spot the stronger summary',prompt:'Classify each statement.',lanes:['Strong summary','Useful detail','Too thin'],items:['Genesis narrows from humanity to Abraham’s family and ends with that family in Egypt.','Joseph becomes powerful in Egypt.','Genesis is about people and God.','Romans develops an argument about sin, grace, Israel, and transformed communal life.'],answer:[0,1,2,0],hints:['A strong summary explains movement and purpose.'],why:'Good summaries expose structure instead of merely naming topics.'}},
'n.people1':{title:'Track people by role, relationship, and change—not by name alone',dek:'Biblical stories become much easier to follow when you know who is related, who holds power, and how those relationships shift.',body:['Names can overwhelm new readers because the Bible introduces families, kings, prophets, priests, disciples, officials, and communities over a long span. A cast list is helpful, but relationships make the list meaningful. Abraham is connected to Isaac; Isaac to Jacob; Jacob to twelve sons. Samuel anoints Saul and David. Nathan confronts David. Elijah confronts Ahab.','Role matters too. A prophet is not merely a “religious person”; prophets often speak into royal and communal power. A priest, king, judge, apostle, governor, and scribe occupy different social functions. The same person can also change roles over time.','When reading narrative, sketch only relationships that affect the scene. The goal is not genealogy trivia. It is understanding who can command whom, who depends on whom, who is in conflict, and whose perspective the story gives you.'],plain:'Names stick when you connect them: family, role, power, conflict, and change.',visual:{type:'relationship',title:'A relationship map carries more information than a cast list',nodes:[{title:'David',role:'King; father; subject of prophetic critique'},{title:'Nathan',role:'Prophet who confronts David'},{title:'Bathsheba',role:'Affected by David’s abuse of royal power; later mother of Solomon'},{title:'Solomon',role:'David’s son and successor'}],note:'Relationships explain why actions carry different power and consequences.'},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Match people with roles',prompt:'Connect each figure with the broad role that best orients a beginner.',items:['Moses','Samuel','David','Elijah'],options:['Liberator/lawgiver in Exodus tradition','Prophet/judge who anoints kings','King of the united monarchy','Prophet who confronts royal power'],answer:[0,1,2,3],hints:['Samuel stands between judges and monarchy.'],why:'Role and relationship turn names into a usable mental map.'}},
'n.people2':{title:'The New Testament cast is a network, not a hero list',dek:'Jesus, disciples, women patrons and witnesses, apostles, local leaders, opponents, and imperial officials all occupy different relationships in the story.',body:['The Gospels center on Jesus but include disciples, crowds, religious leaders, women followers, Roman officials, family members, and marginalized people who respond differently. Acts expands the network to Peter, Stephen, Philip, Paul, Barnabas, Lydia, Priscilla, Aquila, James, local elders, rulers, and many others.','The letters reveal even more names. Phoebe is commended in Romans. Priscilla and Aquila are coworkers and teachers. Timothy and Titus serve in ministry partnerships. Households and house churches matter because early Christian communities meet in social networks rather than purpose-built church buildings.','Tracking these people helps correct an overly simplified picture in which one famous apostle does everything. Early Christianity spreads through a distributed network of witnesses, hosts, coworkers, messengers, teachers, and local communities.'],plain:'The early Church is a network of people and communities. Learn relationships and functions, not just famous names.',visual:{type:'relationship',title:'A distributed early-Christian network',nodes:[{title:'Peter',role:'Jerusalem/apostolic witness'},{title:'Paul',role:'Missionary and letter writer'},{title:'Priscilla & Aquila',role:'Coworkers, hosts, teachers'},{title:'Phoebe',role:'Commended servant/benefactor connected with Romans'},{title:'Local churches',role:'Recipients and active participants'}],note:'The network is illustrative, not exhaustive.'},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'See the network',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['Acts and the letters name many coworkers beyond the apostles.','Households and local communities are important to early Christian life.','Only the most famous male apostles exercise meaningful ministry.','Every named person has the same role and authority.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Look for diversity of roles, not sameness.'],why:'New Testament communities depend on distributed people, gifts, relationships, and local leadership.'}},
'n.all':{title:'Build a reliable profile for any biblical book',dek:'Whole-library content mastery means being able to orient a book quickly without pretending uncertain background details are facts.',body:['For any book, start with six questions: Where is it on the shelf? What kind of writing dominates? What is the broad setting? What happens or what argument develops? Who are the key voices/people? Who is the audience or implied audience?','Then add evidence labels. Some facts are explicit: Romans names Paul and addresses believers in Rome. Some are traditional: the titles of anonymous Gospels preserve longstanding attribution. Some are scholarly reconstructions: proposed dates, sources, or community settings. Keep those categories visible.','Finally, give the reader an entry point. A good profile says why the book matters and what to watch for, not merely chapters, author, and date. The goal is to make first reading more intelligent without replacing the first reading.'],plain:'A good book profile gives location, genre, setting, movement, people, audience, evidence level, and a reason to read.',visual:{type:'book-profile',title:'A complete beginner-friendly book profile',shelf:'Group + neighboring books',setting:'Narrated/historical setting with confidence level',synopsis:'One movement-focused paragraph',people:'Key voices and relationships',audience:'Explicit or reconstructed, labeled clearly',purpose:'What questions/themes to watch while reading'},challenge:{kind:'capstone',title:'Choose the responsible profile',prompt:'Which profile-writing rule is best?',clues:['Some books name senders; others are anonymous.','Traditional attribution and modern reconstruction are different evidence types.','A profile should prepare reading without replacing it.'],options:['Present every background claim with the same certainty.','Separate explicit, traditional, and reconstructed claims and explain the book’s movement.','Avoid all background information.','Summarize only the most famous verse.'],answer:1,hints:['Calibration is part of accuracy.'],why:'A strong profile combines orientation with honest evidence labels.'}}
});
